{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And furthermore regarding 'besides the burnt-offering of the new moon.'</b> Why is it not written here 'besides the sin-offering of the new moon,' which is one goat for a sin-offering? For just as it mentions the burnt-offering, it should have mentioned the sin-offering, since even on Yom HaKippurim we find written 'besides the sin-offering of the atonements.' And the answer: it mentioned the burnt-offering only because its atonement is somewhat equivalent to the burnt-offering of Rosh HaShanah — for the burnt-offering of Rosh Chodesh atones for where there is no awareness neither at the beginning nor at the end, and that of Rosh HaShanah for where there is no awareness at the beginning but there is awareness at the end. However, the truth of the matter is that on Rosh HaShanah there are two musaf goats — one of Rosh HaShanah and one of Rosh Chodesh — and since the verse did not specify the goat of Rosh Chodesh explicitly, the Talmud [ראש השנה, חג] calls it 'a festival that covers the new moon,' for this goat comes for atonement and consolation for the moon which was diminished. And since it is true that two goats are offered for the musaf of Rosh Chodesh, they enacted to say in the musaf of Rosh HaShanah: 'and two goats to make atonement, and the two daily offerings as their law.' And also, it is correct to say 'besides the burnt-offering of the new moon and its meal-offering,' for it too is offered in the musaf. And in the name of the Rav Natan'el I found that one need not say 'and two goats,' for since the verse covers it, one should not publicize it. And some ask: why do we not say in the musaf prayer of Shavuot likewise 'and two goats,' for in Zevachim it concludes: the two goats of Atzeret, what are they for? For tum'ah that occurred between this and that. And likewise the verses prove it in [פרשת] אמור, for it is written 'ועשיתם שעיר עזים לחטאת,' and Rashi comments: 'Could the goat mentioned here be the goat mentioned in Chumash HaPekudim? When you reach the bulls and rams, it is not so.' And further, we can prove that it is not the same, for there it is written 'לחטאת' and here it is not written 'לחטאת.' If so, since two goats are offered on Atzeret and their atonement is equivalent to atone for tum'ah of the Temple and its sacred things like the other musaf goats, it stands to reason to mention them in the prayer. And one may answer: it is not comparable, for the musaf prayer comes in place of the musaf offering, and one of these goats was not offered in the musaf but only in the morning. And so too Rashi explained in [פרשת] אמור: 'from now on, these are for themselves and those are for themselves — these are offered on account of the bread, and those for the musaf.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:28.962205206Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}